---
// SEO Content Sections - Help with search rankings and provide value
// Includes Ohio-specific "Information Gain" content for 2026 SEO requirements

const updatedDate = '2026-07-11';

// HowTo Schema for paint calculation (high-impact SEO for featured snippets)
const howToSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "name": "How to Calculate Paint Coverage",
  "description": "Step-by-step guide to calculate exactly how much paint you need for your painting project, including wall area calculation, deductions for doors and windows, and coating requirements.",
  "image": "https://paintprocalculator.com/og-image.png",
  "totalTime": "PT5M",
  "estimatedCost": {
    "@type": "MonetaryAmount",
    "currency": "USD",
    "value": "0"
  },
  "supply": [
    { "@type": "HowToSupply", "name": "Tape measure" },
    { "@type": "HowToSupply", "name": "Calculator or paint calculator app" },
    { "@type": "HowToSupply", "name": "Notepad for recording measurements" }
  ],
  "tool": [
    { "@type": "HowToTool", "name": "PaintPro Calculator" }
  ],
  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "name": "Measure Room Dimensions",
      "text": "Measure your room's length, width, and ceiling height in feet. Record each measurement.",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "name": "Calculate Wall Area",
      "text": "Use the formula: Wall Area = 2 × (Length + Width) × Height. For a 12' × 14' room with 8' ceilings: 2 × (12 + 14) × 8 = 416 sq ft.",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "name": "Deduct Openings",
      "text": "Subtract door areas (21 sq ft each) and window areas (15 sq ft standard, 50 sq ft for large/sliding). For 1 door and 2 windows: 21 + 30 = 51 sq ft deduction.",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "name": "Calculate Net Paintable Area",
      "text": "Subtract deductions from wall area: 416 - 51 = 365 sq ft net paintable area.",
      "position": 4
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "name": "Determine Gallons Needed",
      "text": "Divide net area by paint coverage rate (400 sq ft/gal standard). Multiply by number of coats (2 recommended). 365 ÷ 400 × 2 = 1.825 gallons. Round up to 2 gallons.",
      "position": 5
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "name": "Add Waste Buffer",
      "text": "Add 10% for touch-ups and coverage variation. Final estimate: 2 gallons for this example room.",
      "position": 6
    }
  ],
  "author": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "ProBuild Digital LLC"
  },
  "datePublished": "2025-12-26",
  "dateModified": updatedDate
};
---

<section id="how-to-calculate" class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 md:py-16">
  <div class="space-y-12">
    <!-- How to Calculate -->
    <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-heading mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="w-8 h-8 bg-primary/10 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-primary font-bold text-sm">1</span>
        How to Calculate Paint Coverage
      </h2>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 shadow-sm border border-border">
        <p class="text-body mb-4">
          Calculating paint coverage is straightforward once you know the formula. First, measure your room's dimensions - length, width, and ceiling height in feet. Then calculate the wall area using this formula:
        </p>
        <div class="bg-light-gray rounded-lg p-4 font-mono text-sm mb-4">
          Wall Area = 2 × (Length + Width) × Height
        </div>
        <p class="text-body mb-4">
          For example, a 12' × 14' room with 8' ceilings has:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-body space-y-1 mb-4">
          <li>Wall area: 2 × (12 + 14) × 8 = <strong>416 sq ft</strong></li>
          <li>After deducting a door (-21 sq ft) and 2 windows (-30 sq ft)</li>
          <li>Net paintable area: <strong>365 sq ft</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-body mb-4">
          Standard paint covers about 400 square feet per gallon. With two coats, you'd need approximately <strong>2 gallons</strong> for this room.
        </p>
        <p class="text-body text-sm">
          Treat the result as a buying estimate. Check the coverage range on the paint you choose before placing the order.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Tips for Accuracy -->
    <article id="tips">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-heading mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="w-8 h-8 bg-primary/10 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-primary font-bold text-sm">2</span>
        Tips for Accurate Paint Estimates
      </h2>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-5 shadow-sm border border-border">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-heading mb-2">Measure Twice</h3>
          <p class="text-body text-sm">
            Double-check your measurements before purchasing. It's easier to buy extra than to make multiple trips to the store.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-5 shadow-sm border border-border">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-heading mb-2">Account for Texture</h3>
          <p class="text-body text-sm">
            Textured walls absorb more paint. If your walls have knockdown, orange peel, or other textures, add 15-20% to your estimate.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-5 shadow-sm border border-border">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-heading mb-2">Consider Color Changes</h3>
          <p class="text-body text-sm">
            Going from dark to light? You may need 3 coats or a tinted primer. Light to dark usually needs standard 2 coats.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-5 shadow-sm border border-border">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-heading mb-2">Don't Forget Trim</h3>
          <p class="text-body text-sm">
            Baseboards, door frames, and crown molding add up. Budget about 1 quart per 100 linear feet of trim.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- When to Use Primer -->
    <article id="primer">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-heading mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="w-8 h-8 bg-primary/10 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-primary font-bold text-sm">3</span>
        When to Use Primer
      </h2>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 shadow-sm border border-border">
        <p class="text-body mb-4">
          Primer isn't always necessary, but it's essential in these situations:
        </p>
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3">
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3 p-3 bg-light-gray rounded-lg">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-accent mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" />
            </svg>
            <span class="text-sm text-body"><strong>New drywall</strong> - absorbs paint unevenly without primer</span>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3 p-3 bg-light-gray rounded-lg">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-accent mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" />
            </svg>
            <span class="text-sm text-body"><strong>Stained surfaces</strong> - primer blocks water stains and smoke damage</span>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3 p-3 bg-light-gray rounded-lg">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-accent mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" />
            </svg>
            <span class="text-sm text-body"><strong>Dark to light colors</strong> - helps achieve true color in fewer coats</span>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3 p-3 bg-light-gray rounded-lg">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-accent mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" />
            </svg>
            <span class="text-sm text-body"><strong>Glossy surfaces</strong> - primer helps paint adhere better</span>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3 p-3 bg-light-gray rounded-lg">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-accent mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" />
            </svg>
            <span class="text-sm text-body"><strong>High-humidity rooms</strong> - mold-resistant primer protects bathrooms</span>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3 p-3 bg-light-gray rounded-lg">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-accent mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" />
            </svg>
            <span class="text-sm text-body"><strong>Previously unpainted wood</strong> - seals and prevents tannin bleed</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-body mt-4 text-sm">
          <strong>One caution:</strong> A paint-and-primer product does not replace a dedicated primer on every surface. Check the label for the surface you are coating.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article id="regional-insights">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-heading mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="w-8 h-8 bg-accent/10 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-accent font-bold text-sm">4</span>
        What I learned painting my Ohio home
      </h2>
      <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-accent/5 to-primary/5 rounded-xl p-6 shadow-sm border border-accent/20 space-y-4">
        <p class="text-body">
          The calculator handles geometry. It cannot see a damp basement wall, a rough block surface, or a room that is slow to dry on a muggy afternoon. Those conditions mattered more than the arithmetic when I was buying paint for my own house.
        </p>
        <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-4 border border-border">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-heading mb-2">Let the product instructions set the schedule</h3>
          <p class="text-body text-sm">On humid days, I give a coat more time instead of treating the shortest recoat time as a deadline. If the wall still feels cool or tacky, it is not ready. A fan or dehumidifier can help, but the label on the can remains the starting point.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-4 border border-border">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-heading mb-2">Check the forecast for exterior work</h3>
          <p class="text-body text-sm">Ohio weather can move quickly in spring and fall. I check the application temperature on the product, then look beyond the afternoon high to the overnight low and the chance of rain.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-4 border border-border">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-heading mb-2">Treat bare block as its own project</h3>
          <p class="text-body text-sm">Concrete block used more coating than smooth drywall in my basement. Moisture also has to be solved before paint goes on. For masonry, use the product's stated coverage range and expect the rough surface to land near the low end.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- HowTo Schema for Featured Snippets -->
<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(howToSchema)} />
